<section id="work" aria-label="Research and working together">
  <div>
    <h2>It started with floorball footage</h2>
    <p>
      My first computer vision projects were about tracking players across
      cameras. The questions have grown since then: how do we understand a
      person we can only partly see, or recognise a pose from an unfamiliar
      angle?
    </p>
    <p>
      That work became <a href="/publications/#probpose">ProbPose</a> at CVPR and
      <a href="/publications/#bbox-mask-pose">BBox-Mask-Pose</a> at ICCV.
      <a href="/publications/#repogen">RePoGen</a> received the Best Poster Award
      at FG 2024. I share the code, models and demos so others can build on them.
    </p>
    <p class="page-links">
      <a href="/publications/">Papers &amp; theses</a><a href="/work/"
        >Projects &amp; code</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h2>Research, put to use</h2>
    <p>
      Some of my work happens with people outside computer vision: tools for
      <a href="/work/#facis">police analysts</a>,
      <a href="/work/#infant-sensorimotor">infant-development researchers</a> and
      <a href="/work/#revie">floorball coaches</a>. Our forensic work received
      the Czech Ministry of the Interior's award for excellent research results.
    </p>
    <p>
      At CTU, I also <a href="/about/#teaching"
        >teach and supervise student research</a
      >. One of those projects, SAM-pose2seg, won Best Student Paper at CVWW
      2026. Reviewing is another part of that life; I was an Outstanding
      Reviewer at CVPR 2025.
    </p>
    <p class="page-links">
      <a href="/cv/#recognition">Awards &amp; full background</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
